# Claude Code Instructions

This is an **nr-workbench** project for neutron reflectometry analysis at
{{ facility }} {{ instrument }}. It was scaffolded by `nrw init` and follows the
shared AI-assisted workflow below.

@.github/copilot-instructions.md

## Read this first

**If this project already has fits in it, start with one command:**

```bash
nrw handoff <sample>
```

It prints the whole state of play — the escalations a previous session left,
how that session ended, the integrity check, what has been fitted, who is
reading, and what to read in what order. It exists because rebuilding that
picture by hand has been measured at 28 tool calls, and everything in it is
derivable. `skills/reflectometry/analyst-handoff/SKILL.md` is the standard for
what to do with it.

If `nrw` is not found, the handoff tells you how to invoke it here. **Do not go
looking for the binary** — that search has cost ten tool calls before now.

`skills/reflectometry/nr-workbench-project/SKILL.md` is the map of this project:
where files belong, which command does what, and the provenance rules. Read it
before creating a file or running a fit.

## Skills

Skills live in [`skills/`](skills/) at the repository root — tool-neutral, so
both Claude Code and GitHub Copilot reach them by `Read`. Neither tool
auto-discovers that folder, so each skill has a **thin dispatcher agent** in
[`.claude/agents/`](.claude/agents/) and `.github/agents/` whose only job is to
load the skill.

**To change a standard, edit its `SKILL.md` — never the dispatcher.**

`nrw skills sync` reinstalls the bundled skills after a package upgrade. A
skill you have edited locally is never silently replaced — the new version is
written alongside as `.nrw-new` for you to merge.

## Provenance rules

These are what make this project different from a folder of scripts. Breaking
them is how you end up unable to say which script produced a figure.

1. **Never hand-edit a generated fit script.** `models/<name>.py` is derived
   from `models/<name>.yaml`. Edit the spec and regenerate. If the spec cannot
   express what you need, run `nrw model fork` — that keeps full provenance.
2. **Never overwrite a result.** Every fit writes a new immutable directory
   under `samples/<id>/results/<fit_id>/`.
3. **No absolute paths in committed files.** Everything resolves from the
   project root.
4. **No boolean toggles inside a fit script.** Two variants means two specs,
   each with its own hash and its own records.
5. **Run `nrw check` before calling anything final.**

## Write down what you learn, next to what it is about

The analysis is the thinking, not the fits. Record it as you go, scoped to what
it is about:

| A finding about… | Goes in | Written with |
|---|---|---|
| one run | `samples/<id>/results/<fit_id>/NOTES.md` | `nrw note <fit_id> -m "..."` |
| how the fits relate | `samples/<id>/reports/*.md` | `nrw note --sample <id> --title "..." -m "..."` |
| the tooling, the instrument, a project-wide convention | [`docs/ground_truths.md`](docs/ground_truths.md) | an editor |

**If a finding names a run number or a fit id, it belongs to the sample** — not
to `docs/ground_truths.md`, which is for what is true regardless of which
sample you are looking at.

Cite fit ids in the prose. That is the whole linking mechanism: `nrw ls`, the
fit page and `nrw pack` all find a report by the ids it mentions.

After a fit, `nrw assess <fit_id>` writes the automatic checks — parameters on
their bounds, unconstrained posteriors, BIC — into its `NOTES.md`, and adds a
language model's reading if an endpoint is configured. Run it, then add the
sentence it cannot write: what you were testing, and what you now believe.

**A fit you are abandoning still needs a note saying why.** That sentence is
worth more than the fit and is the first thing forgotten.

## Commands

**Setting up and looking around**

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| **Take over an analysis in progress** | `nrw handoff <ID>` |
| Check the environment | `nrw doctor` |
| Make bare `nrw` work in assistant sessions | `nrw doctor --fix-path` |
| Say who is reading, so reports are pitched right | `nrw audience --ask` |
| Add a sample | `nrw sample new <ID>` |
| See what data is actually on disk | `nrw sample scan <ID>` |
| Browse everything in a browser | `nrw serve` |

**Understanding the data before modelling it**

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Assess a tNR run | `nrw tnr assess data/tnr/<dir> --label <label>` |
| One metric at a time | `nrw tnr amplitude\|variogram\|chi2\|qbands\|pca <dir>` |

Read the `assessment.json` verdict before choosing a constraint form — it
reports whether anything is changing, whether one reaction coordinate explains
it, and whether the change looks like a thickness or a contrast.

**Modelling**

`nrw model new` fills in the facts — runs, files, and each segment's incident
angle read from its `# Meta:` header. It cannot fill in the stack. Run it with
`--print-prompt` to get the exact instruction for that, including which skills
to read; `--from-notes` does the same thing against a configured LLM endpoint.

Whichever route: **never edit `states`, `series`, `thetas`, `data_dir` or
`run`.** Those are measured values, not defaults. In particular do not "tidy"
a theta of 1.201 to 1.2 — it came from the file.

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Start a spec from what the scan found | `nrw model new <ID> --name <name>` |
| ...and let an LLM propose the stack | `nrw model new ... --from-notes` |
| ...or get the instruction to do it yourself | `nrw model new ... --print-prompt` |
| Check a spec without fitting | `nrw model validate <spec.yaml>` |
| Build it in-process and report the parameter table | `nrw model preview <spec.yaml>` |
| Write the refl1d script | `nrw model generate <spec.yaml>` |
| Take manual ownership of a script | `nrw model fork <script.py>` |

**Fitting and provenance**

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Run a fit (spec-generated or hand-written) | `nrw fit run <script.py>` |
| List fits | `nrw ls` |
| Trace a figure, `.par`, or data file to its fit | `nrw whence <path>` |
| Compare two fits | `nrw diff <fit_a> <fit_b>` |
| Bless a result | `nrw promote <fit_id> --as final --reason '...'` |
| Verify project integrity | `nrw check` |

`nrw serve` also exposes `/api/…`, which returns exactly what the browser
renders — useful for reading a curve, an assessment, or a fit's provenance
without opening a page.

Run `nrw --help` for the full surface.
